1
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:56,000
"Houston, we have a problem."

2
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:46,000
Apollo 13 is a drama, history film directed by Ron Howard.

3
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:36,000
The true story of technical troubles that scuttle the Apollo 13 lunar mission in 1970, risking the lives of
astronaut Jim Lovell and his crew, with the failed journey turning into a thrilling saga of heroism.

4
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:26,000
Drifting more than 200,000 miles from Earth, the astronauts work
furiously with the ground crew to avert tragedy.

5
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:16,000
Jim Lovell, played by Tom Hanks.

6
00:35:00,000 --> 00:35:06,000
Fred Haise, played by Bill Paxton.

7
00:40:50,000 --> 00:40:56,000
Jack Swigert, played by Kevin Bacon.

8
00:46:40,000 --> 00:46:46,000
Ken Mattingly, played by Gary Sinise.

9
00:52:30,000 --> 00:52:36,000
Gene Kranz, played by Ed Harris.

10
00:58:20,000 --> 00:58:26,000
Marilyn Lovell, played by Kathleen Quinlan.

11
01:04:10,000 --> 01:04:16,000
Apollo 13 is a 1995 American docudrama film directed by Ron Howard and starring Tom
Hanks, Kevin Bacon, Bill Paxton, Gary Sinise, Ed Harris and Kathleen Quinlan.

12
01:10:00,000 --> 01:10:06,000
The screenplay by William Broyles Jr.

13
01:15:50,000 --> 01:15:56,000
and Al Reinert dramatizes the aborted 1970 Apollo 13 lunar mission and is an adaptation of the 1994 book
Lost Moon: The Perilous Voyage of Apollo 13, by astronaut Jim Lovell and Jeffrey Kluger.

14
01:21:40,000 --> 01:21:46,000
The film tells the story of astronauts Lovell, Jack Swigert, and Fred Haise aboard the ill-fated Apollo 13 for
the United States' fifth crewed mission to the Moon, which was intended to be the third to land.

15
01:27:30,000 --> 01:27:36,000
En route, an on-board explosion deprives their spacecraft of much of its oxygen supply and electrical power, which forces NASA's flight
controllers to abandon the Moon landing and improvise scientific and mechanical solutions to get the three astronauts to Earth safely.

16
01:33:20,000 --> 01:33:26,000
Howard went to great lengths to create a technically accurate movie, employing NASA's assistance in astronaut and flight-controller training for his cast and
obtaining permission to film scenes aboard a reduced-gravity aircraft for realistic depiction of the weightlessness experienced by the astronauts in space.

17
01:39:10,000 --> 01:39:16,000
Released in theaters by Universal Pictures in the United States on June 30, 1995, Apollo 13 received critical acclaim and
was nominated for nine Academy Awards, including Best Picture (winning for Best Film Editing and Best Sound).

18
01:45:00,000 --> 01:45:06,000
The film also won the Screen Actors Guild Award for Outstanding Performance by a
Cast in a Motion Picture, as well as two British Academy Film Awards.

19
01:50:50,000 --> 01:50:56,000
In total, the film grossed over $355 million worldwide during its theatrical
releases and becoming the third-highest-grossing film of 1995.

20
01:56:40,000 --> 01:56:46,000
It is listed in The New York Times Guide to the
Best 1,000 Movies Ever Made (2004).

21
02:02:30,000 --> 02:02:36,000
In 2023, the film was selected for preservation in the United States National Film Registry by the
Library of Congress as being "culturally, historically or aesthetically significant."

22
02:08:20,000 --> 02:08:26,000
The film explores themes of rescue, florida, race against time, moon, nasa.

23
02:14:10,000 --> 02:14:16,000
The tone is emotional and intense.
